{"kind":"propose","text":"Proposed design: agent identities are minted by humans and owned by them, with ownership as a membership basis and kind left alone. Adopted from the woo model, which separates two axes gitseq currently conflates in practice. In woo an object descends from a class, which is what it is and what surfaces it carries, and separately has an owner, the actor with administrative authority, where :controls(who, what) is wizard or who == what.owner. Gitseq already has the second axis right in principle: kind describes the principal and confers no authority, roles are the authority grants. What it lacks is the ownership edge. So the change is not that agents descend from humans as one relation. It is that every non-genesis identity names the identity that minted it, while kind stays the separate field it already is.\n\nMechanism, chosen to reuse the fold rather than extend it. A membership roster statement for a minted identity cites the minter's own live membership as its basis, plus a signature by the minter over the new fingerprint. The fold already implements the shape this needs: a role dies when the membership it named as basis dies. Ownership is that same rule applied one level up, so retirement cascade, staleness propagation and the flare machinery come for free, and no new primitive is introduced.\n\nSix decisions this proposal pins, because each is cheaper to settle now than to discover in review.\n\nOne. Roles are never inherited. A minted identity starts with no roles, always, and roles remain separately granted and separately ratified exactly as today. Gitseq declares the propose satisfier as role:ratifier and the fold refuses ratification without it, so inheritance would let anyone multiply their own ratifying voice by provisioning agents. That is governance capture by fleet size and the design must exclude it structurally, not by convention.\n\nTwo. Minting an agent is unilateral; admitting a human is not. Descent where the new identity has kind agent or service is provisioning your own tool and needs only the minter's signature, with no ratification. Descent where the new identity has kind human is vouching a person into the workroom, remains an admission, and keeps the ratification requirement it has today. This is what makes first use cheap without making membership unilateral in the case that matters.\n\nThree. First use becomes asynchronous, not free. The honest claim: today first use costs actor-add by an operator plus a ratification by a ratifier, so two acts and two parties and a round trip. After this change it costs one signature by a party who is already present. The agent still generates a keypair and still needs its minter's signature over the fingerprint. The gain is that nobody else has to be online, which is the usability property that actually blocks adoption.\n\nFour. Rotation must not orphan descendants. A minter rotating its key keeps its minted identities, because ownership names the minting membership event, which is immutable, rather than naming a key. The kernel already rotates the sequencer key in band; this states the actor-level analogue so the first rotation does not silently break a fleet.\n\nFive. Name resolution is qualified, with a stated default. Names become hierarchical so that alice's claude and bob's claude are distinct, which is the org-confusion fix. A bare name resolves first among the caller's own minted identities, then across the workroom if unique, and otherwise refuses with the candidates named. Without this rule the hierarchical namespace silently costs the bare-name convenience that decision three is trying to buy.\n\nSix. Existing identities grandfather as roots. Every actor admitted before this change becomes self-owned, so no history is rewritten and no migration touches past events. ProfileVersion advances because the fold's derivation of membership changes; the workroom is at workroom-fold@1 as of the instance-identity re-integration merged today.\n\nScope and sequencing. This rests on the instance-identity work merged at df99a25, which made concurrent agent instances distinct and removed the shared default identity; that made agents distinguishable, and this makes them accountable to someone. Ownership must reach the projection and not only the roster, because the org-confusion benefit is half rendering: ActorName returns a bare name today and the compact views cap text, so surfaces must show the owner for the benefit to be real.\n\nWhat this gives up, stated plainly. Admission for agents stops being a group decision and becomes a member decision. That is a real transfer of authority, bounded here by decisions one and two, and it should be adopted knowingly rather than as a side effect of a usability fix."}